The shebang line is the first line of a script that tells the OS which interpreter to use:

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash   # portable — finds bash wherever it is installed
#!/bin/bash           # absolute path — only works if bash is at /bin/bash
```

`#!/usr/bin/env bash` is preferred because it works on systems where bash is installed in a non-standard location (e.g. macOS with Homebrew bash at `/usr/local/bin/bash`).

```bash
parse_name() {
    local full="$1"
    echo "${full%% *}"   # print first name (strip from first space to end)
    echo "${full#* }"    # print last name  (strip up to first space)
}

# Capture both outputs with process substitution
read -r first last < <(parse_name "Alice Smith")
echo "First: $first"   # Alice
echo "Last:  $last"    # Smith
```

Alternative: use global variables (less clean):
```bash
parse_name() {
    FIRST_NAME="${1%% *}"
    LAST_NAME="${1#* }"
}
parse_name "Alice Smith"
echo "$FIRST_NAME $LAST_NAME"
```

```bash
factorial() {
    [[ $1 -le 1 ]] && echo 1 && return
    echo $(( $1 * $(factorial $(( $1 - 1 ))) ))
}

factorial 5   # 120
factorial 0   # 1
```

Note: Bash recursion is slow because each function call spawns a subshell for `$()`. For performance-critical code, prefer iteration or use a compiled language.
